WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.160
This is the VOA Special English Development Report.

00:05.160 --> 00:12.600
Member countries of the International Criminal Court met in June in Kampala, Uganda.

00:12.600 --> 00:18.120
They were there to examine the court's progress for the first time.

00:18.120 --> 00:25.000
Observers, human rights activists, and civil society groups also attended the two-week

00:25.000 --> 00:27.120
review conference.

00:27.120 --> 00:35.720
A treaty called the Rome Statute established the court in the Hague in the Netherlands in 2002.

00:35.720 --> 00:43.840
The court can try people for genocide, war crimes, and crimes against humanity when their

00:43.840 --> 00:49.280
own countries are unwilling or unable to.

00:49.280 --> 00:57.800
The court was also given the right to try crimes of aggression, although it never has.

00:57.800 --> 01:06.040
This is partly because ICC members could not agree on how to define aggression.

01:06.040 --> 01:11.220
In Kampala, they agreed on a compromise resolution.

01:11.220 --> 01:18.320
It defines the crime in terms of acts by a political or military leader against

01:18.320 --> 01:24.680
another state in violation of the United Nations Charter.

01:24.680 --> 01:33.320
Invasions, attacks, and blockades could all be tried as acts of aggression.

01:33.320 --> 01:43.240
So could letting another country use a state's territory for aggressive acts against a third state.

01:43.240 --> 01:50.640
The United Nations Security Council has the lead responsibility for deciding that an act

01:50.640 --> 01:54.400
of aggression has taken place.

01:54.400 --> 02:01.560
But if the council takes no action within six months, the court might still be able

02:01.560 --> 02:05.640
to carry out an investigation.

02:05.640 --> 02:12.560
Critics pointed out that the agreement does not permit the court to punish aggression

02:12.560 --> 02:17.080
by non-member countries or their nationals.

02:17.080 --> 02:25.640
Also, members could refuse to accept the court's right to try crimes of aggression.

02:25.640 --> 02:34.600
ICC countries also agreed to delay any action by the court on aggression until they reexamined

02:34.600 --> 02:38.240
the issue in seven years.

02:38.240 --> 02:46.320
The International Criminal Court has yet to complete its first case involving any crime.

02:46.320 --> 02:51.440
Eleven cases are currently before the court.

02:51.440 --> 02:57.880
They include the case against Sudanese President Omar al-Bashir.

02:57.880 --> 03:05.520
He is the first head of state to be charged by the ICC while in office.

03:05.520 --> 03:11.840
Two years ago, the court found him guilty of war crimes in Darfur.

03:11.840 --> 03:15.600
Last year, it ordered his arrest.

03:15.600 --> 03:18.880
But Sudan rejects the ruling.

03:18.880 --> 03:25.600
The ICC Review Conference took place even as the president was just sworn in for a

03:25.600 --> 03:28.080
new term.

03:28.080 --> 03:33.840
One hundred eleven countries are parties to the Rome Statute.

03:33.840 --> 03:42.160
The United States is not among them, but took part in Kampala as an observer.

03:42.160 --> 03:49.520
Other countries that have not joined the court include China and Russia.

03:49.520 --> 03:53.520
And that's the VOA Special English Development.

